// Chapter 5, "Performance." Sources read for this chapter (verified 2026-10-11):
// Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge, FAA-H-8083-25C, chapter 10 (weight and balance: effects of weight, CG limits, terms, computational
//   method, the Cessna 172 style sample loading problem with stations 34 to 46, 82 to 108 and 108 to 142, AC 43.13-1 negligible weight change),
//   chapter 11 (standard atmosphere, pressure altitude, density altitude, humidity, climb, ceilings, ground effect, takeoff and landing factors:
//   21 percent weight increase needs 10 percent more lift-off speed, 10 percent weight increase gives at least 21 percent more takeoff distance,
//   headwind of 10 percent of takeoff speed cuts takeoff distance about 19 percent, tailwind of 10 percent adds about 21 percent, landing distance
//   about 3.5 percent per 1,000 feet, 10 percent excess landing speed gives at least 21 percent more landing distance, hydroplaning 9 x root of tire
//   pressure), and chapter 5 (effects of forward and aft CG): https://www.faa.gov/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/faa-h-8083-25c.pdf
// Aircraft Weight and Balance Handbook, FAA-H-8083-1B (2016), chapter 2 basic weight and balance equation (weight shifted, delta CG):
//   https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/FAA-H-8083-1.pdf
//   and its MOSAIC addendum dated October 20, 2025, which the FAA says is part of the current edition until 8083-1B is revised:
//   https://www.faa.gov/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/Weight_Balance_HB_Addendum_(MOSAIC).pdf
// Airplane Flying Handbook, FAA-H-8083-3C, chapter 6 (check POH performance before going to the airplane, ground effect on takeoff, airplanes that
//   lift off at high density altitude and can't climb out of ground effect, pick a point on the runway where you should be airborne, takeoff plus
//   landing ground rolls as an accelerate and stop estimate):
//   https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/airplane_handbook/07_afh_ch6.pdf
// Private Pilot Airplane ACS, FAA-S-ACS-6C, PA.I.F Performance and Limitations (K1, K2a to K2f, K3, R1 to R3, S1, S2):
//   https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/acs/private_airplane_acs_6.pdf
// 14 CFR 91.9 and 91.103(b) via the eCFR versioner API, Title 14 as of 2026-10-07: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91
// FAA Type Certificate Data Sheet 3A12, Rev. 84, Model 172N: 2,300 lb normal category, 2,000 lb utility; CG range +38.5 to +47.3 at 2,300 lb and
//   +35.0 to +47.3 at 1,950 lb or less, straight line variation; seats 2 at +34 to +46 and 2 at +73; baggage 120 lb at +95; fuel 42 gal total,
//   40 usable, at +48; empty weight includes unusable fuel and full oil; datum lower front face of firewall. Copy read at
//   https://dyzz9obi78pm5.cloudfront.net/app/image/id/615b6a6626e5f007327b23d1/n/3a12-rev-84.pdf (linked from resources.univair.com/docs/tcds-for-cessna)
// KAPA runway lengths (17L/35R 10,001 ft, 17R/35L 7,001 ft, 10/28 4,800 ft) and field elevation 5,884.9 ft: https://www.airnav.com/airport/KAPA
// NOT VERIFIED, so presented only as Biscuit's own paperwork in the story: I could not read a published 1978 172N POH. Biscuit's basic empty
//   weight (1,486 lb at +39.5 from a fictional 2019 report), the front seat arm of +37.0 (inside the TCDS +34 to +46 range and the PHAK sample),
//   the takeoff distance table, and the climb table are illustrative, internally consistent, and modeled on the format of Cessna POH tables.
//   They are not real 172N figures. The table notes (lean for max RPM above 3,000 ft, 10 percent per 9 knots of headwind, 10 percent per 2 knots of
//   tailwind, 15 percent of ground roll on dry grass) follow the wording style of Cessna POHs. Abby's 50 percent margin is her personal rule.
// All weather, METARs, and people's weights are fictional. Dates assume August 2026, MDT = UTC minus 6. Climbs near KAPA and over Bennett stay
// below 8,000 MSL (Denver Class B floor) unless cleared.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).pilot=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot.full||[]).push({
  num: 5, part: `Part One: The Share`, title: `Performance`, weeks: `August`,
  tasks: [`I.F`,`IV`,`IX`],
  cast: [
    [`Ellie Reyes`,`Danny's daughter, 9. Has been promised a ride in her dad's airplane for most of her life.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `The first week of August, Aurora ran out of shade. Jordan worked a Monday shift that started at seven in the morning with a roofer who'd stopped sweating, and her last call ended at three the next morning with a man in his eighties who'd fallen asleep in his garage with the door down and a box fan blowing hot air at him. Somewhere in the middle her partner said the truck's thermometer read 101, and neither of them laughed, because they'd already been to two of those calls and were about to go to a third.`,
    `Heat is a protocol. You cool them, you watch the pressure, you don't let them stand up too fast. She'd done it a hundred times. What she'd never done was think about what the same afternoon did to a 1978 Cessna sitting on the ramp at Centennial with its wings in the sun.`,
    `Tuesday night, slept out and showered, she was eating cold noodles on the couch when Claire called. Claire didn't call. Claire texted, in full sentences, with periods.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Ellie wants to go up in the airplane,"</span> Claire said, without saying hello. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"She's been asking since she was six. Danny always said when she's tall enough. She measured herself on the pantry door last night. She marked it with a Sharpie."</span> A pause. Jordan could hear the dishwasher running on the other end. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"If Abby flies it. If you're in it. And if she goes, I go. That's the deal."</span>`,
    `Jordan put the noodles down. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Okay."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Saturday the fifteenth. I'm on days all week. Three o'clock is the only time I've got."</span> Another pause. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Don't make it a thing. I'll change my mind."</span>`,
    `After she hung up Jordan sat there for a while. Then she texted Abby, and the answer came back before she'd set the phone down.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="9:51 PM">I can do the 15th at 3. Four of us, August, 3 in the afternoon. You're going to do the numbers. All of them. Thursday 8am, briefing room. Bring Biscuit's POH from the seat back and a pencil.</prop>`,
    `Wednesday she drove to the airport after her nap and got the POH out of the seat back pocket. Biscuit was tied down in her spot on the ramp with the sunshade in the windshield and the yoke lock in, and the cabin was hotter than the back of the ambulance. The book was soft at the corners, held together with clear packing tape along the spine. Section 5 was the most worn. On the takeoff distance page somebody had drawn a pencil box around the 8,000 foot row in the 2,300 pound block, and written next to it in small block capitals: <b>THIS IS AUGUST.</b>`,
    `She knew whose capitals those were.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Abby's Ground School: what the heat takes away`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Pressure altitude: start with the number the airplane feels`,
        body: [`Eight AM and the window unit's already losing. Sit. POH, Section 5, open, and it stays open. Everything today lives in there. So do half the performance questions on your written, because the FAA's favorite move is a chart, three numbers, and a stopwatch.`,
          `First number. Pressure altitude. Your altimeter's a barometer somebody painted feet on. It's set up for the standard atmosphere: 29.92 inches and 15°C at sea level, losing about 2°C a thousand feet. Local setting in the little window, it reads roughly your height above sea level. Put 29.92 in the window instead and it reads pressure altitude. That's height above the standard datum plane, a made up level where the pressure would be 29.92. The charts are built on that one.`,
          `Three ways to get it, per the PHAK. Twist 29.92 into the window and look. Use an E6B. Or the math, which is what I want from you. Field elevation, plus 29.92 minus the setting, times 1,000. Down low an inch of mercury is about a thousand feet, so a hundredth is about ten. This morning: 30.06. 29.92 minus 30.06, minus 0.14. Times 1,000, minus 140. 5,885 minus 140, 5,745. High pressure, the airplane thinks she's lower than she is. Low pressure, higher. The sign is where people bleed points on the written. Get it backward and you can get it backward with total confidence.`,
          `Write the pressure altitude down before anything else. Every chart in that section wants it, and none of them want your field elevation.`],
        terms: [[`Pressure altitude`,`Height above the standard datum plane, where pressure is 29.92 inches. Read it with 29.92 set in the altimeter, or compute it.`],[`Pressure altitude formula`,`Field elevation + (29.92 minus altimeter setting) × 1,000.`],[`Standard atmosphere`,`15°C and 29.92 inches at sea level, cooling about 2°C (3.5°F) per 1,000 feet.`]],
        exam: `Expect to compute pressure altitude from an elevation and an altimeter setting. A setting above 29.92 gives a pressure altitude below field elevation. A setting below 29.92 gives one above it.` },
      { h: `Density altitude, and why August is a different airport`,
        body: [`Pressure altitude assumes the standard temperature. It's August. The temperature is not standard. Density altitude is pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature, and it's the altitude in the standard atmosphere where the air would be as thin as the air you've actually got. The PHAK says it in one sentence I want you to remember: regardless of the actual altitude, the airplane performs as though it were operating at the density altitude.`,
          `The rule of thumb is density altitude equals pressure altitude plus 120 times the difference between the outside air temperature and the standard temperature at that pressure altitude. Standard temperature is 15 minus 2 for every thousand feet. At 6,000 feet that's 15 minus 12, or 3°C. Three degrees. That's what standard is up here. Today's forecast high is 34. So at our pressure altitude this afternoon, call it about 5,900, standard is about 3, the real air is 31 degrees hotter than that, and 31 times 120 is about 3,700 feet. Add it on, and Biscuit is going to take off from Centennial feeling like she's at about 9,600 feet. She's never seen 9,600 feet in her life without almost four thousand feet of climbing first.`,
          `The common mistake is using 15°C as the standard temperature at every altitude. That's only true at sea level. Up here it'll make your density altitude look about 1,400 feet better than it is, and that's the wrong direction to be wrong.`,
          `High elevation, low pressure, high temperature, and high humidity all push density altitude up. Low elevation, high pressure, cold, and dry pull it down. Humidity is the sneaky one. Water vapor is lighter than air, so moist air is less dense than dry air. There's no rule of thumb or chart for it, and the PHAK says humidity alone usually isn't a big factor, but it still costs you some performance, so when it's hot and sticky expect a little less than the chart says. We mostly don't have that problem in Colorado. We have the other three.`],
        terms: [[`Density altitude`,`Pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature. The altitude the airplane performs as though it's at.`],[`Density altitude rule of thumb`,`DA ≈ PA + 120 × (OAT minus standard temperature at that PA).`],[`High density altitude`,`Thin air. Caused by high elevation, low pressure, high temperature, high humidity.`]],
        exam: `Know which conditions raise density altitude and that higher density altitude means worse performance. For the math, the standard temperature has to be computed at the pressure altitude, not left at 15°C.` },
      { h: `What thin air does to the engine, the prop, and the wing`,
        body: [`The PHAK lists three things you lose. Power, because the engine takes in less air with every breath. Thrust, because the propeller has less air to bite. And lift, because thin air pushes on the wing with less force at any given speed.`,
          `Biscuit's engine has no supercharger, so it starts losing power the moment it leaves sea level, and it's already lost a lot of it sitting here. The mixture matters more than it does anywhere else. The carburetor was set up for sea level air, so at a high field full rich is too rich, and too rich at full throttle is less power. That's why the POH note says to lean for maximum RPM in a full throttle runup before takeoff from fields above 3,000 feet. You know this from Marisol's hangar. Today you're going to feel it.`,
          `Now the wing, which is where people get confused. The wing needs the same dynamic pressure as always, so she lifts off at her usual indicated airspeed. Fine. But in thin air that indicated number comes with a bigger true airspeed, so more groundspeed when the wheels leave. You need more speed and you've got less engine to make it with. The airspeed indicator looks totally normal. The runway edge lights go by like you're on I-25. Landing's the same deal in reverse. Same indicated numbers, more speed over the concrete at touchdown, longer rollout.`,
          `Climb hurts worst. Climb is excess power: what's left after you've paid the bill for staying level. Thin air cuts your paycheck and leaves the bill alone. Absolute ceiling, nothing left, zero feet a minute. Service ceiling, 100 feet a minute and no better. Both of Biscuit's got a lot lower since breakfast.`],
        terms: [[`Excess power`,`Power available minus power required for level flight. It's what makes rate of climb.`],[`Absolute ceiling`,`The altitude where maximum rate of climb is zero.`],[`Service ceiling`,`The altitude where the airplane can't climb faster than 100 feet per minute.`],[`True airspeed at altitude`,`Same indicated airspeed, higher true airspeed in thin air, so a higher groundspeed at lift-off and touchdown.`]],
        exam: `At high density altitude the airplane lifts off and lands at the same indicated airspeed but a higher true airspeed and groundspeed. Takeoff roll goes up, climb rate goes down, landing roll goes up.` },
      { h: `Reading the takeoff table`,
        body: [`Now the page with the pencil box on it. Biscuit's takeoff table is a short field table, which means the numbers assume the short field technique in the book, flaps 10, full throttle before you let off the brakes, on a paved, level, dry runway with no wind. Cessna gives you blocks by weight, rows by pressure altitude, and columns by temperature. Each box has two numbers. Ground roll, and total distance to clear a 50 foot obstacle. The second one is the one that keeps you out of the trees.`,
          `Read the conditions line at the top of every chart before you read a single number in it. The PHAK warns that manufacturers don't present this stuff the same way. Some charts are built on pressure altitude, some on density altitude, some are tables and some are graphs, and the numbers mean nothing if you put the wrong thing in.`,
          `When your conditions land between rows or columns, you interpolate. That just means if you're halfway between two numbers, you take the number halfway between them. If you're 40 percent of the way from 30°C to 40°C, you go 40 percent of the way from the 30 degree distance to the 40 degree distance. Weight and pressure altitude, I want you to round up to the next block and the next row instead of interpolating. Up, always, toward the worse number. And a chart is a test pilot in a new airplane on a good day. Biscuit is 48 years old and you're a student. That's what the ACS means when it asks about differences between calculated and actual performance.`,
          `Then the notes under the table, which are the part people skip. Ours say three things. Lean for max RPM at full throttle before takeoff above 3,000 feet. Decrease the distances 10 percent for each 9 knots of headwind, and for tailwinds up to 10 knots, increase them 10 percent for each 2 knots. And on dry grass, add 15 percent of the ground roll figure. Look at the wind numbers again. Headwind gives you a little back. Tailwind takes a lot more away. The PHAK says the same thing in general terms: a headwind that's 10 percent of your takeoff speed cuts the distance about 19 percent, and a tailwind that's 10 percent of your takeoff speed adds about 21 percent. Upslope runways are longer for takeoff. Downslope runways are shorter for takeoff and longer for landing, and the Chart Supplement tells you the slope.`,
          `My own rule, and it's mine, not the FAA's: take the corrected distance over 50 feet and add half again. If that number doesn't fit the runway, we don't go. 91.103 says you have to know your runway lengths and your takeoff and landing distance data before every flight. It doesn't say how much room to leave. I do.`],
        terms: [[`Ground roll`,`Distance from brake release to lift-off.`],[`Total distance over a 50 foot obstacle`,`Ground roll plus the air distance to climb to 50 feet. The number that keeps you clear of obstacles.`],[`Interpolation`,`Estimating a value proportionally between two chart entries.`],[`91.103(b)`,`For every flight, the PIC must know runway lengths and the takeoff and landing distance data.`]],
        exam: `Expect a table or graph, a set of conditions, and a wind correction from the chart notes. Read the conditions line first. Headwind credit is small and tailwind penalty is large.` },
      { h: `Weight: the number you control`,
        body: [`You can't do much about the weather. You can do a lot about weight. The PHAK has a list of what too much weight costs you, and it's long. Higher takeoff speed. Longer takeoff run. Worse climb rate and climb angle. Lower ceiling, shorter range, slower cruise. Higher stall speed. Faster approach. Longer landing roll. And it says the first sign that an airplane's overloaded usually shows up during the takeoff.`,
          `Here's the part that should scare you a little. A heavier airplane needs more lift to leave the ground, so it needs more speed. The PHAK says a 21 percent increase in weight takes a 10 percent increase in lift-off speed. And because distance goes with the square of speed and the extra mass also slows the acceleration, a 10 percent increase in takeoff weight costs you at least a 21 percent increase in takeoff distance. Landing is gentler on paper. A 10 percent heavier airplane needs about 10 percent more landing distance. But a 10 percent fast approach, at any weight, costs you at least 21 percent more landing distance. Wind works the same way it does on takeoff: a tailwind that's 10 percent of your landing speed adds about 21 percent, and at our approach speeds a 10 knot tailwind is more than that. Landing distance also grows about 3.5 percent for every thousand feet of altitude, which is about 20 percent before you've even left our ramp.`,
          `Now the legal side. Maximum weight is an operating limitation, and 91.9 says nobody operates an aircraft without complying with the operating limitations in its flight manual, markings, and placards. For a 172N that's 2,300 pounds in the normal category. It's on the type certificate. The PHAK also says something nobody puts on a bumper sticker: an airplane certified for a maximum weight may not be able to take off safely at that weight under all conditions. High, hot, and humid may require a reduction in weight before flight is attempted. The maximum is a ceiling, and on some days it's a ceiling you shouldn't get anywhere near.`],
        terms: [[`Maximum weight`,`An operating limitation from the type certificate and POH. 2,300 lb for a 172N in the normal category. 91.9 requires compliance.`],[`Useful load`,`Pilot, passengers, baggage, usable fuel, and drainable oil. Maximum weight minus basic empty weight.`],[`Weight and takeoff distance`,`About a 10 percent weight increase costs at least 21 percent more takeoff distance.`]],
        exam: `Know the PHAK list of overweight effects and the classic percentages: 21 percent more weight needs 10 percent more lift-off speed, 10 percent more weight needs at least 21 percent more takeoff distance, 10 percent extra landing speed needs at least 21 percent more landing distance.` },
      { h: `Balance: arms, moments, and the box you have to stay in`,
        body: [`Weight is how much. Balance is where. The center of gravity is the point the airplane would balance on if you could hang it from one string, and it has to be inside a range the manufacturer set. Every location in the airplane is measured from a datum, which is just a reference line Cessna picked. For a 172N it's the lower front face of the firewall. The distance from the datum to an item is its arm, in inches, plus if it's behind the datum. Multiply weight by arm and you get its moment, in pound inches. Add up all the weights. Add up all the moments. Divide total moment by total weight, and that's your CG. That's the whole method. The PHAK walks through it with a table, and so do I.`,
          `Biscuit's numbers come from three places. The type certificate data sheet says the 172N seats sit two at 34 to 46 inches, which is why Biscuit's paperwork uses 37 for the front seats, and two at 73. Baggage is 120 pounds max at 95 inches. Fuel is 40 usable gallons at 48 inches, and usable fuel weighs 6 pounds a gallon. Her basic empty weight and its arm come from her own weight and balance report, the current one, in the airplane's records. Not the sample in the POH. The PHAK says sample loading problems are for guidance only, because every airplane gets modified and every airplane is different. Ours includes the unusable fuel and full oil, so you don't add those.`,
          `The CG range on the type certificate for a 172N is 35.0 to 47.3 inches at 1,950 pounds or less, and 38.5 to 47.3 inches at 2,300 pounds, with a straight line between them. So the aft limit stays put and the forward limit slides aft as the airplane gets heavier. That's the envelope. The POH draws that as a box with one corner cut off. Weight up the side, moment or CG along the bottom, make a dot. Dot's inside at takeoff and inside at landing or you don't go. Biscuit's fuel sits a few inches behind where her CG usually lives, so burning it slides the dot forward a little, about an inch at most. Plenty of airplanes move a lot more.`,
          `Dot's outside? Move something. The weight and balance handbook has one formula and you can flip it around for any unknown. Weight shifted over total weight equals change in CG over distance moved. Or the way I use it: CG change equals weight shifted, times distance, divided by total weight. Say a 190 pound guy moves from the back seat up front. That's 36 inches. Airplane weighs 2,286. 190 times 36 is 6,840, divided by 2,286, about 3 inches forward. Huge. People are the heaviest thing you get to move around.`],
        terms: [[`Datum`,`The reference line all arms are measured from. For the 172N, the lower front face of the firewall.`],[`Arm`,`Distance in inches from the datum. Positive aft.`],[`Moment`,`Weight × arm, in pound inches. Often divided by 1,000 to make an index.`],[`CG`,`Total moment ÷ total weight.`],[`Weight shift formula`,`ΔCG = weight shifted × distance shifted ÷ total weight. Rearrange it for any unknown.`]],
        exam: `Expect a loading problem: fill in weight, arm, and moment, total them, divide, and check the envelope. Expect a weight shift problem solved with the 8083-1B formula. Use 6 pounds per gallon for avgas.` },
      { h: `Forward CG, aft CG, and how each one bites`,
        body: [`Biscuit's tail pushes down. Everybody's surprised by that. The CG normally sits a little ahead of the center of lift, so she wants to drop her nose, and the horizontal tail fights it by pushing down. Which means the wing is lifting the airplane plus whatever the tail's pushing.`,
          `Forward CG, the tail pushes harder. More load on the wing. The PHAK: stalls at a higher speed. More back pressure, and way up at the front of the range you can run out of elevator in the flare and land on the nosewheel, which Marisol will hear about. The forward limit's usually picked for landing. Forward is stable and heavy and a touch slow in cruise from the tail drag.`,
          `Aft CG means less tail download, so a little less drag and a little more cruise speed. That's the good news. The bad news is it's less stable, the control forces get light, which makes it easy to overstress the airplane by accident, and stall recovery gets harder. Spin recovery gets harder too, and far enough aft you can get a flat spin that won't recover. The PHAK says an airplane that recovers fine from a spin at one CG can fail to recover when the CG moves aft by an inch or two. Out of the two, aft of the limit is the one that kills people.`,
          `There's a second category on the type certificate for a reason. A 172N in the utility category has a lower weight, 2,000 pounds, and a much tighter aft limit, because utility maneuvers need it. We're in the normal category every day, and we stay there.`],
        terms: [[`Forward CG`,`More stable, higher stall speed, more elevator needed, heavier flare, slightly slower cruise.`],[`Aft CG`,`Less stable, lighter control forces, slightly faster cruise, harder stall and spin recovery.`],[`Tail download`,`The downward force from the horizontal tail that balances a CG ahead of the center of lift.`]],
        exam: `Know the forward and aft CG lists. Forward: higher stall speed. Aft: reduced stability and harder stall and spin recovery.` },
      { h: `Climbing, ground effect, and the point where you quit`,
        body: [`Two climb speeds. VX, best angle, gets you the most altitude for the distance over the ground, which is what you want with trees at the end of the runway. VY, best rate, gets you the most altitude for the time, which is what you want the rest of the time. In a propeller airplane VX is slower than VY, and both are in the POH. The AFH says a 5 knot miss from either one can cost a lot of climb, so you fly them, not near them.`,
          `Ground effect is the trap. When the wing is close to the runway, within about one wingspan, the ground squashes the wingtip vortices and induced drag drops. At a quarter of the span up, it's about 25 percent less induced drag. At a tenth of the span, about half. So a heavy airplane on a hot day can lift off below the speed it needs, float along in ground effect feeling fine, and then climb out of it into thin air with the drag coming back all at once. The AFH says it plainly: at high density altitude, high temperature, or maximum weight, an airplane may lift off and not be able to climb out of ground effect. If that happens, you don't pull. You lower the nose a little, stay in ground effect, and let it accelerate to climb speed. Pulling is how people stall twenty feet off the runway.`,
          `Last thing, and it's the one I care about most. Before every takeoff, the AFH says to pick a point on the runway where you should be airborne. If you get there and you aren't, you pull the power to idle, brake, and keep it straight. One more thing from that chapter: the takeoff ground roll plus the landing ground roll from the POH is a good estimate of the runway you need to speed up and then stop. On a 10,000 foot runway it'll feel silly. Do it anyway. It's the habit you want the day you're somewhere with 4,000 feet and a fence.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"And lean the thing,"</span> from the doorway. Marisol, coffee, no idea how long she's been there. Correct, though. Full rich at this elevation in August and you get to meet a much smaller engine.`],
        terms: [[`VX`,`Best angle of climb. Most altitude per distance. Clears obstacles.`],[`VY`,`Best rate of climb. Most altitude per unit of time.`],[`Ground effect`,`Reduced induced drag within about one wingspan of the surface. Can let an airplane lift off before it can climb.`],[`Abort point`,`A point on the runway, picked before takeoff, where the airplane should be airborne. If it isn't, reject the takeoff.`]],
        exam: `Ground effect reduces induced drag near the surface, and an airplane lifted off too early may not climb out of it at high density altitude. Know VX versus VY, and the AFH advice to choose a point on the runway to be airborne by.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f5s1`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`Pressure first`, q:`Field elevation is 5,885, and the one o'clock altimeter forecast is 29.90. What's the pressure altitude?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 8:10 Abby wrote the morning's two lines on the whiteboard. The air conditioner in the briefing room window was running full blast and doing about as much good as a dog panting at the sun.`,`<prop type="doc" title="KAPA, Thursday 6:53 AM, and the forecast for 1 PM"><code>METAR KAPA 061253Z 18004KT 10SM SKC 17/07 A3006 RMK AO2<br><br>Forecast for 1900Z: wind 330 at 10, temperature 34°C, altimeter 29.90</code></prop>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"We're going to fly at one this afternoon, on purpose, in the heat, so you know what you're asking Biscuit for next Saturday. Pressure altitude at one o'clock. Show me how you got it."</span>`,`Jordan had the POH open in front of her, the pencil box on the 8,000 foot row staring up at her.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`5,905. Add twenty feet to field elevation for the lower setting.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Yep. Low pressure, the airplane thinks it's higher than it is."</span> She writes 5,905 in the corner of the board in green marker and boxes it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Everything this afternoon starts from that box. Don't make me erase it."</span>`,
          why:`Pressure altitude = field elevation + (29.92 minus altimeter setting) × 1,000. 5,885 + (0.02 × 1,000) = 5,905. A setting below 29.92 puts pressure altitude above field elevation.`},
        {t:`5,865. Subtract twenty feet from field elevation for the lower setting.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Backward."</span> She draws an arrow pointing up. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Lower pressure is what you'd find higher up. So you add. Every time."</span>`,
          why:`The sign is the whole question. 29.92 minus 29.90 is plus 0.02, so you add 20 feet. Subtracting puts the airplane 40 feet better off than it is, and on a hot day the error always adds up in the wrong direction.`},
        {t:`About 5,885. The setting is close enough to 29.92 to skip the math.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Today, close enough. In a front, with 29.50, it's four hundred feet."</span> She writes 5,905 anyway. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Do the math every time, so you don't have to remember which days it matters."</span>`,
          why:`Twenty feet doesn't change much today. But pressure altitude is a computed number, and skipping the step is a habit. At 29.50 the same shortcut is off by 420 feet.`},
        {t:`Set 29.90 in the window, and read the altimeter on the ramp.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That gives you field elevation, give or take the instrument."</span> She taps the 29.92 on the board. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Pressure altitude is what it reads with this in the window."</span>`,
          why:`With the local altimeter setting dialed in, the altimeter reads close to field elevation. Pressure altitude is what it reads with 29.92 set in the Kollsman window, or what you compute from the formula.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s2`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`Thirty one degrees above standard`, q:`At 34 degrees Celsius and a 5,905 foot pressure altitude, what's the density altitude, and what does it mean?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Next. Density altitude, one o'clock, and then tell me what it means for us in real words. Not test words."</span>`,`Jordan worked it on the legal pad. Standard temperature at 5,905 feet. Then the difference. Then times 120. She checked it twice like a drip rate, then once more because Abby was watching.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`About 9,600. Biscuit will perform like she's up at 9,600 feet.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Yep."</span> She writes it under the box. 9,600. Then she writes 5,885 next to it and draws a long arrow between them. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Call it thirty seven hundred feet of mountain we never had to climb. Engine, prop, wing, all three of them think they're up there. The runway's the only thing still at 5,885."</span>`,
          why:`Standard at 5,905 is 15 minus 11.8, about 3.2°C. 34 minus 3.2 is 30.8. 30.8 × 120 is about 3,700. 5,905 + 3,700 is about 9,600 feet. The airplane performs as though it's at the density altitude.`},
        {t:`About 8,200, with 15°C as standard. Biscuit performs like she's at 8,200.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Fifteen is standard at the beach."</span> She writes 3°C next to 6,000 on the board. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Up here standard is three. You just gave yourself fourteen hundred feet of free airport."</span>`,
          why:`Standard temperature has to be figured at the pressure altitude: 15 minus 2 per 1,000 feet. Using 15°C at 5,905 understates density altitude by about 1,400 feet, which makes every chart number look better than it is.`},
        {t:`About 9,600. That's what the altimeter reads after takeoff.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The altimeter doesn't know it's August."</span> She points at the window, at Biscuit on the ramp. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"It'll read what it always reads. The shelf's still at eight. Density altitude is how she flies, not where she is."</span>`,
          why:`Density altitude describes performance, not indicated altitude. With the altimeter set to the local setting it still shows your height above sea level. The Class B floor at 8,000 MSL doesn't move on a hot day.`},
        {t:`About 9,600. The engine makes less power, but the wing works the same.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Engine, yes. Wing, no."</span> She draws a wing. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Same indicated airspeed to fly, higher true airspeed to get it. So more runway and less climb. It's all three of them. Engine, prop, and wing."</span>`,
          why:`The number is right. But thin air also costs the propeller thrust and the wing lift at a given true airspeed. The airplane lifts off at its normal indicated airspeed, which is a faster true airspeed and groundspeed at high density altitude.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s3`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`The old sheet`, q:`Danny's card says Biscuit's empty is 1,438 at 39.1. Which number should Jordan trust?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tucked into the back of the POH, behind Section 6, was an index card in a plastic sleeve. Danny's capitals again. <b>BISCUIT W&B. EMPTY 1,438 @ 39.1. ME 185. FULL FUEL 240.</b> Under that, a column of loading combinations he'd worked out and a smiley face next to the bottom one.`,`Jordan held it up. Abby didn't take it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Before you use anything on that card, go ask Marisol where she keeps the airplane's records. Then decide which empty weight you believe."</span>`,`Marisol was at her bench in the hangar with a carburetor in pieces on a shop towel. She looked at the card, and something went across her face and came back.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The latest weight and balance report in the maintenance records.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Marisol pulls a binder off the shelf over the bench and opens it to a tab marked W&B. On top is a form with her own signature on it, dated October 2019. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"ADS-B box, the panel GPS, the antenna, the wiring. I weighed her on scales after. 1,486 at 39.5."</span> She looks at the card in Jordan's hand. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"That card's from before. He never redid it. I told him to."</span> She closes the binder and hands it over. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Forty eight pounds, she's gained. Same as Walt since 1978."</span>`,
          why:`The current weight and balance record for this specific airplane is the only empty weight that counts. Equipment changes alter both weight and CG, and the PHAK says those changes must be recorded. An old card, even a careful one, describes an airplane that no longer exists.`},
        {t:`Danny's card, the same numbers he flew Biscuit on for years.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Marisol taps the 1,438 with a greasy fingernail. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Worked is a strong word. That airplane's gone. I added forty eight pounds to it with my own hands."</span>`,
          why:`Having gotten away with it isn't data. The card predates the 2019 equipment installation, so its empty weight and arm are both wrong for the airplane on the ramp today.`},
        {t:`The empty weight from the sample loading problem in the POH.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"That's an example."</span> She flips the POH to the sample and taps the word on top of it. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"It says sample. It's somebody else's airplane."</span>`,
          why:`The PHAK says the sample loading problems in a POH are for guidance only, and each aircraft has to be treated separately. The empty weight comes from the airplane's own weight and balance record.`},
        {t:`Danny's card, plus about twenty pounds for the radios and the GPS.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Right instinct. Wrong number."</span> She gets the binder down. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"It's forty eight, and the arm moved too. Don't guess when it's written down."</span>`,
          why:`You noticed the card was out of date, which is the hard part. But an estimate of the change misses both the weight and the shift in CG. The current report already has the measured answer.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s4`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`Two of us and forty gallons`, q:`Two people, full tanks, bags and water are on board this afternoon. How should Jordan do weight and balance?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Back in the briefing room, with the binder, Abby slid the blank worksheet across the table. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"This afternoon. You, me, full tanks, our two flight bags and a gallon of water in the back, because it's August and I'm not dying of heatstroke over Bennett. Weight and balance. Go."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I'm one fifty with boots on. I'll say it once."</span>`,(s.flags.rushedpreflight||G.rushedpreflight)?`Jordan thought about the preflight items she'd skipped in May because Marisol had signed for them, and she didn't love how fast her brain was already reaching for the shortcut.`:`Jordan had a pencil in her hand and the urge, which she noticed and didn't like, to say two people is obviously fine.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Skip the worksheet. Two people and full fuel always fit in a 172.`, s:0, flag:`skippedwb`, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Abby doesn't argue. She writes ALWAYS on the whiteboard and draws a circle around it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That word's in more accident reports than any other one. Saturday there are four of us. Will that be always too?"</span> Jordan does the worksheet after all, quietly, while Abby gets more coffee.`,
          why:`Two front seat occupants and full fuel usually fits in a 172, and that's exactly why it's a bad habit to skip. 91.9 makes the limitations mandatory, and the only way to know you're inside them is to compute them. The same shortcut on a heavier day is how airplanes get loaded over gross.`},
        {t:`Total the weights and check the sum against the 2,300 pound limit.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Half a worksheet."</span> She points to the slanted corner of the envelope in the POH. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Weight's one number. Where it sits is the other one. Some days the airplane's light and still out of balance."</span>`,
          why:`Weight is only half of it. The CG has to fall inside the envelope too, and the forward limit moves with weight. It takes the moments to know.`},
        {t:`Run the full worksheet with standard weights of 170 per person.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Standard weights are for when you don't have real ones."</span> She points at herself. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I told you mine. Use it. Same for yours."</span>`,
          why:`The PHAK says standard weights shouldn't be used if actual weights are available. Here they are, so use them.`},
        {t:`Work the moment for each item and check the CG against the envelope.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She does it in pencil, in neat columns, the way she'd chart vitals. 2,058 pounds. 83,661 pound inches. 40.65. She checks the forward limit at that weight and gets 36.08. Inside, with room. Abby reads it upside down and doesn't say anything, which is how Jordan knows it's right. Then she says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Now keep that sheet. Saturday you'll add two people to it."</span>`,
          why:`The computational method: list weight and arm, compute each moment, total the weights and moments, divide for CG, and check it against the limits at that weight. It takes four minutes and it's the only way to know.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s5`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`The page with the box on it`, q:`At 2,058 pounds, 5,905 feet and 34 degrees, which chart entries should Jordan use?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Now the takeoff table. 2,058 pounds, 5,905 feet, 34 degrees. Tell me which numbers you're going to use and why."</span>`,`<prop type="doc" title="Biscuit's POH, Section 5. Short field takeoff distance"><code>FLAPS 10°. FULL THROTTLE PRIOR TO BRAKE RELEASE. PAVED, LEVEL, DRY RUNWAY. ZERO WIND.<br>GROUND ROLL / TOTAL TO CLEAR 50 FT OBSTACLE, FEET<br><br>2300 LB&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;20°C&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;30°C&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;40°C<br>5000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1175/2120&nbsp;&nbsp;1265/2290&nbsp;&nbsp;1360/2475<br>6000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1290/2350&nbsp;&nbsp;1390/2550&nbsp;&nbsp;1495/2760<br>7000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1420/2620&nbsp;&nbsp;1530/2850&nbsp;&nbsp;1645/3090<br>8000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1565/2935&nbsp;&nbsp;1690/3200&nbsp;&nbsp;1815/3480<br><br>2100 LB&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;20°C&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;30°C&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;40°C<br>5000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;955/1750&nbsp;&nbsp;1030/1890&nbsp;&nbsp;1105/2040<br>6000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1050/1935&nbsp;&nbsp;1130/2095&nbsp;&nbsp;1215/2270<br>7000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1155/2155&nbsp;&nbsp;1245/2340&nbsp;&nbsp;1340/2535<br>8000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1275/2410&nbsp;&nbsp;1375/2620&nbsp;&nbsp;1480/2845<br><br>NOTES: 1. Prior to takeoff from fields above 3000 ft elevation, lean the mixture for maximum RPM in a full throttle, static runup.<br>2. Decrease distances 10% for each 9 knots headwind. For operation with tailwinds up to 10 knots, increase distances by 10% for each 2 knots.<br>3. For operation on a dry, grass runway, increase distances by 15% of the "ground roll" figure.</code></prop>`,`In pencil, off to the side of the 8,000 foot row in the 2,300 pound block, was the box and the word AUGUST.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The 2,100 block at 6,000, interpolating 34 between 30 and 40°C.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Why 2,100 and not 2,058?"</span> Jordan doesn't look up. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Next heavier block. Round toward worse."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"And why interpolate the temperature?"</span> Jordan, still not looking up: <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Because thirty four's a real number and I've got it."</span> Abby nods once and gets up to refill her coffee. The machine has smelled like burnt toast since Jordan's first lesson in April. Over her shoulder she says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Weight and altitude, round up. Temperature you can work. Now do the wind, and don't look at me while you do it, I'm not going to make a face."</span> She makes a face anyway when Jordan writes cosine, but it's the good face, the one Jordan's seen maybe twice.`,
          why:`Round weight and pressure altitude to the next more demanding entry, so 2,100 lb and 6,000 feet. Interpolate temperature proportionally: 34°C is 40 percent of the way from 30 to 40. Then apply the wind note.`},
        {t:`The 2,100 block at 5,000, entering the rows with field elevation.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"5,905 is ninety five feet from 6,000."</span> She lets that sit on the table for a while, next to the coffee rings. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"And field elevation isn't on this chart anywhere. Go look. Pressure altitude is."</span>`,
          why:`The table is entered by pressure altitude, and 5,905 is just under the 6,000 row. Dropping to the 5,000 row takes about 215 feet off the 50 foot number (about 100 off the ground roll), all of it in your favor and none of it real.`},
        {t:`The 2,100 block at 6,000, but round 34 down to the 30°C column.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Rounds down. In August."</span> She writes 34 on the board. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"If you're not going to interpolate, the only acceptable direction is up, to the 40 column. Never toward the nicer number."</span>`,
          why:`Rounding toward the easier condition makes the chart lie in your favor. Either interpolate or use the more demanding column, which here is 40°C.`},
        {t:`The 2,300 block at 8,000 and 40°C, the worst box on the page.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Safe. Useless for learning, but safe."</span> She almost smiles, and taps the pencil box with the back of her pen. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's your brother's method, I'm pretty sure. He boxed that row for a reason, and I'd bet you a burrito he never read the other ones. Today read the real one."</span>`,
          why:`Using the worst case on the page is conservative and never unsafe for a go or no go. But it throws away what you know, and the test wants you to read the chart for the actual conditions.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s6`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`IV`, title:`Where she quits`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 12:50 the ramp was shimmering like a parking lot in a truck commercial, and it was only getting worse. The tie down ropes were hot enough that Jordan untied them with her sleeves pulled down over her hands, and when she got in, the yoke was too hot to hold, so she sat there with the door open and the little air vents blowing on it until she could. Somebody two rows over was washing a Bonanza and the water was drying on the wing before he could get the chamois to it. The ATIS said wind three two zero at one zero, temperature three four, altimeter two niner niner zero, runway three five right. She'd already turned the temperature into a density altitude on the legal pad in her lap.`,`The run up went fine. She leaned for max RPM at full throttle like the note said, and watched the tach climb as she did it, which was a strange thing to watch, a number getting better because she'd taken something away. At the hold short line, Abby said:`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Before you go. Where do you quit?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Nowhere special. With ten thousand feet, there's room for anything.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Always."</span> Abby isn't smiling. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"If the engine's going to quit, I'd like it to quit while we're rolling on pavement and not when we're two hundred feet over somebody's trampoline. Pick a spot."</span>`,
          why:`A long runway doesn't replace a decision point. The AFH says to identify a point on the runway where you should be airborne, so that a sick engine or poor acceleration gets caught while there's still pavement to stop on.`},
        {t:`At the taxiway 1,500 feet down if they're still on the ground.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Good. Say it out loud on the roll."</span> So she does. Throttle in, brakes off. Biscuit gets going like she's wading. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Airspeed alive. Forty. Taxiway coming."</span> Off before it by a few hundred feet. Jordan had wanted more.`,
          why:`The chart says about 1,050 feet of ground roll with the headwind, so 1,500 feet gives the airplane a fair chance and leaves more than 8,000 feet to stop. Choosing an abort point you can see, with plenty of runway left to stop, turns a vague worry into one decision you've already made. If you reach it on the ground, power to idle, brake, and stay straight.`},
        {t:`At the far end, if they're still on the ground and not making power.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The far end is where you learn you should've quit a mile back. Try again."</span>`,
          why:`An abort point at the end of the runway leaves no room to stop. The point has to come early enough that you can reject the takeoff on the remaining pavement without heroic braking.`},
        {t:`Wherever the airspeed starts to feel too slow to her on the roll.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Slow compared to what, March?"</span> She points out the windshield at 10,000 feet of gray runway, the far end of it going soft and wobbly in the heat. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Everything's slow today. Pick something you can actually see. A taxiway, a distance remaining sign, a crack in the concrete, whatever. Then your stomach doesn't get a vote."</span>`,
          why:`Watching airspeed is right. But a hot day makes everything feel sluggish, so a feeling isn't a standard. A point you chose ahead of time, from the chart, is.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s7`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`IV`, title:`Twenty feet`, q:`Biscuit lifted off two knots slow and is floating just off the runway. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Takeoff number two was off 35L, the short one, 7,000 feet, because Abby wanted Jordan to see a runway end that wasn't a rumor. Jordan pulled back early. Not by much. She knew it right away, same as she knew a missed IV before the flash chamber stayed empty. Biscuit lifted, then sat there five feet off the concrete like she was thinking it over. One chirp from the stall horn. The airspeed was two knots under the grease pencil tick Abby had put on the glass that morning, the one Marisol was going to gripe about cleaning off. Runway left, sure, plenty. Past that the fence, and a road, and a business park full of beige roofs cooking in the sun, and that was about it for options.`,`Abby's hands were in her lap. They stayed there.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Lower the nose a little and build up speed before she climbs.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Jordan puts the nose down about two degrees. She hates it. Some lizard part of her brain hates it for the full two seconds, and two seconds on that clock is about a week. The horn quits. The needle creeps up to the grease pencil. Then she lets the nose come up and Biscuit climbs out over the fence and the road and the business park, slow as anything, while some guy in a parking lot stops loading his truck to watch. Probably wondering if he should call somebody. Abby breathes out. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Ground effect. Thirty six feet, give or take, about one wingspan. Then it's gone. You did fine."</span> GE on the kneeboard, underlined twice. Nobody talks again until the reservoir, which is fine with Jordan.`,
          why:`Lifting off below the recommended speed at high density altitude can leave an airplane flying in ground effect but unable to climb out of it. The AFH fix is to lower the pitch, accelerate in ground effect to climb speed, here VX for the fence, then climb.`},
        {t:`Ease back and get her climbing before the runway end gets closer.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`The horn goes from a chirp to a steady whine. Abby shoves the yoke forward over Jordan's hands and holds it there till the horn shuts up, hard enough that Jordan's right wrist is still sore on Saturday. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's how people stall at twenty feet with a runway right under them. Speed first. Then height."</span> Not another word out of her until they've landed and turned off. Then, flat: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Again."</span>`,
          why:`Raising the nose without enough airspeed increases drag and angle of attack, near the ground, in thin air. It's a recipe for a stall at an altitude with no room to recover.`},
        {t:`Hold the attitude where it is and let her climb on her own.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Not much. The airspeed goes up a knot and stays there. The fence is getting closer. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Waiting works in January. Today you have to get the speed. Nose down a little."</span>`,
          why:`Holding a pitch attitude that's slightly too high leaves the airplane stuck near the ground with marginal airspeed. A small pitch reduction lets it accelerate while still in ground effect.`},
        {t:`Pull the power to idle, and put her back on the runway that's left.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"If she was sick, sure, we have the runway for it. She's not sick. She wants five more knots."</span>`,
          why:`Putting it back down is a reasonable choice if there's runway and something's wrong with the airplane. Here the problem is technique, an early rotation, and the fix is to accelerate in ground effect. Landing back isn't wrong, just not needed.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s8`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`Nose heavy`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`They climbed east to 7,500 under the shelf. It took a while. Abby had her time it, and it was about 520 feet a minute with just the two of them, which Jordan wrote on her kneeboard under the 5,905 and the 9,600, because she had a feeling she was going to want all three of those numbers again on Saturday. Out past the reservoir the plains were brown already, a month early, and the wheat that was left had been cut into stubble that threw heat up at them in bumps you could see coming only because the dust devils marked them. Over Bennett it got bumpy enough that her pencil came off the kneeboard twice. A water bottle rolled around the floor the whole lesson. Hers, probably.`,`On the way back in, somewhere over the reservoir, Abby said, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Feel how much back pressure she wants? That's us two and a gallon jug. Saturday you've got Claire and Ellie back there instead of a jug. What changes?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The CG goes aft. She'll be less stable, and lighter in pitch.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Yep. Also about a knot faster in cruise. Nobody at the club's ever noticed that, not even Russ, who'd spot a free knot from across the street."</span> She taps the trim wheel between the seats with the toe of her boot. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Today we're nose heavy, so you've been cranking in nose up trim all the way down final like you're winding a clock. Saturday, less. Trim it like today and she'll balloon on you right over the numbers with Claire watching."</span>`,
          why:`An aft CG reduces the tail's download. The airplane is less stable, control forces get lighter, stall and spin recovery gets harder, and cruise speed rises a little. A forward CG gives a higher stall speed and heavier elevator forces.`},
        {t:`The CG moves aft. That makes her more stable and steadier in pitch.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Other way around."</span> She tries to balance her pencil on one finger to show her. It lasts about a second, then drops and rolls under the seat to go live with the water bottle. She leaves it. Not worth the seatbelt. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Tail heavy's less stable. That's the one that bites. Ask Walt about the Cherokee Six sometime. Actually, don't."</span>`,
          why:`Moving the CG aft decreases longitudinal stability. Spreading weight along the fuselage doesn't add stability. The PHAK says the aft CG limit is set largely by stability.`},
        {t:`The CG goes aft, and her stall speed goes up with it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Heavier, yes, so stall speed goes up some. That's the weight. The CG goes the other way. Forward CG is what raises stall speed. Two separate things."</span>`,
          why:`More weight does raise stall speed. But the CG effect is separate: a forward CG raises stall speed, and an aft CG lowers it slightly while costing stability. Keep weight and balance as two different questions.`},
        {t:`Not much. The rear seats are near the CG, so trim and feel stay put.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Seventy three inches is a long way from forty."</span> She reaches back and slaps the rear seat cushion. A little puff of 1978 comes up out of it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Two people back there move the CG more than all the fuel does."</span>`,
          why:`The rear seats are at +73, well aft of a typical loaded CG around +40 to +44. Weight there moves the CG aft noticeably, which is why every passenger has to go on the worksheet.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s9`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`Tired`, q:`Ellie wants to know if Biscuit gets tired on hot days. What should Jordan tell her?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday it was 97 in Parker and Claire's air conditioner was making a noise like a dentist's drill in the next room. The three of them ended up on the kitchen floor in front of the open freezer, eating grape popsicles, because Ellie said that was the rule over 95 and nobody could prove otherwise.`,`Ellie knew about Thursday. Ellie knew about all of it. Claire knew about all of it, from Abby, by text. Jordan filed that away to be weird about later.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Mom says Biscuit had a hard time getting up because it was hot."</span> Ellie got the drip on the side of her popsicle with her tongue, mostly. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Does the airplane get tired?"</span>`,`Over Ellie's head Claire gave Jordan a look. Her own popsicle was running down into her watchband and she wasn't doing a thing about it. It was the June look, from the trampoline, the clouds question. Your turn.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Kind of. Hot air is thinner, so she has to work harder to fly in it."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Ellie chews on that. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Like when you run at the top of a mountain."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Exactly like that. Same lungs, less air in every breath. Engine, propeller, wings, all of it runs on air, and on a 97 degree day there's less of it to go around."</span> Ellie licks the stick, considering. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"So she's not tired. She's out of breath."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Light, a little sheepish.">"Okay, that's better than how I said it."</span> Ellie finishes the popsicle, very pleased with herself, and talks around the stick: <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Is she going to be out of breath on Saturday?"</span> Claire gets up and shuts the freezer. Then she just stands there with her back to them, a little too long.`,
          why:`Hot air is less dense. The engine makes less power, the propeller makes less thrust, and the wing makes less lift at a given true airspeed, so the airplane needs more runway and climbs slower. Out of breath is close to perfect.`},
        {t:`"No. Machines don't get tired if you take care of them."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Then why did it have a hard time?"</span> Which is fair. Jordan doesn't have an answer that fits inside the one she just gave.`,
          why:`It's true that the airplane isn't wearing out. But it doesn't perform the same every day. Performance depends on air density, and hot days make it worse.`},
        {t:`"Kind of. Hot air weighs more, so she has more of it to lift."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Claire, from the floor, without looking up: <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"I'm pretty sure it's the other way."</span> It is.`,
          why:`Hot air is less dense, not heavier. That's the whole problem. There's less air for the engine, the prop, and the wing to work with.`},
        {t:`"Kind of. Her engine gets too hot, and then she can't push as hard."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"So Saturday she'll be broken?"</span> Ellie's face starts to go somewhere Jordan doesn't want it to go. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"No. No. Not broken."</span>`,
          why:`Engine temperatures matter on a hot day, and you watch them. But the main reason for poor takeoff and climb is thin air reducing power, thrust, and lift, not an overheating engine.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s10`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`The softball team`, q:`Russ's softball loading sheet is under gross weight. What should Jordan tell him?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday evening Walt was in the hangar when she came by, sitting on Marisol's stool with his glasses on the end of his nose and a sheet of paper on his knee. He didn't say hello. He held it out.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Russ is taking two guys from his softball team to Greeley Sunday morning for golf. He did his own numbers. He says they work. Check them."</span>`,`<prop type="doc" title="Russ's loading sheet, Sunday"><code>EMPTY&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1486 @ 39.5&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;58,697<br>RUSS (FRONT)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;180 @ 37&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;6,660<br>MIKE (BACK)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;190 @ 73&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;13,870<br>DEV (BACK)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;190 @ 73&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;13,870<br>CLUBS&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;120 @ 95&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;11,400<br>FUEL 20 GAL&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;120 @ 48&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;5,760<br>TOTAL&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2286&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;110,257<br>CG = 48ish. UNDER GROSS. GOOD TO GO. (the guys like the back, more legroom)</code></prop>`,`Jordan did the division on her phone. 48.23. She opened the POH to the envelope. Aft limit 47.3, at every weight.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him he's fine to go. He's under max gross at 2,286.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Walt takes the paper back and folds it once, slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Under gross and an inch aft. You know what an inch aft does in a stall?"</span> He doesn't wait. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Neither does Russ."</span>`,
          why:`Being under maximum weight doesn't make the loading legal. A CG of 48.23 is nearly an inch aft of the 47.3 limit, which means reduced stability and harder stall and spin recovery. That's the dangerous side of the envelope.`},
        {t:`Tell him to move one passenger up front, into the right seat.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She shows Walt the work. 190 pounds times 36 inches, divided by 2,286. 2.99. New CG 45.24. Inside, with room. Walt reads it, then reads the division again. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Send it to him. Not me. You."</span> She does. Russ answers in four minutes: <b>wait the back is NOT more legroom??</b> Then: <b>ok dev sits up front. he's going to want the window. this is a nightmare. thank you.</b> Walt stands up and puts the stool back where Marisol keeps it, exactly, and on his way out he says, <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"That's twice now."</span> She doesn't know what the first time was.`,
          why:`Weight shift formula from FAA-H-8083-1B: ΔCG = weight shifted × distance ÷ total weight. Moving a 190 lb passenger from +73 to +37 is 36 inches: 190 × 36 ÷ 2,286 = 2.99 inches forward, to 45.24. Weight is unchanged and the CG is back inside the limits.`},
        {t:`Tell him to burn off some fuel first, and the CG will move forward.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Walt shakes his head. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Fuel's at 48. His CG's at 48 and change. Burn it off and he goes further aft, not forward."</span>`,
          why:`Burning fuel removes weight at +48. With the CG already at +48.23, removing weight forward of the CG moves the CG aft. And the loading has to be legal at takeoff, not after.`},
        {t:`Tell him to strap the golf bags into the empty front seat.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"The math works."</span> He thinks about it. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Three golf bags next to the yoke and the rudder pedals. Russ. I'll pass."</span>`,
          why:`Moving 120 lb from +95 to +37 shifts the CG about 3.04 inches forward, so it works on paper. But loose bulky cargo in the front seat can interfere with the controls. Moving a person is the cleaner fix.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s11`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`IX`, title:`A hundred RPM`, q:`The tach reads about a hundred RPM low, and her abort point is coming up. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday the 13th was hotter than the week before. Abby wanted one more hot takeoff before Saturday, then pattern work, then out. At the hold short Jordan did the run up and the mag check, and the radio got busy, and the tower cleared them for takeoff with a Citation on a three mile final.`,`Full throttle. Brakes off. Something was off. The tach was lower than last week. Not a lot. Maybe a hundred RPM, and with the tach reading fifty high to begin with, she didn't trust her own math. The airplane was rolling, just slowly. Her abort point, a taxiway she'd picked off the diagram, was coming up, and the airspeed needle was a long way from where it had been at that taxiway last Thursday.`,`Her eyes went to the red knob. All the way in. Full rich. She'd pushed it in for the mag check and never pulled it back.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep the power in and lean the mixture while she rolls.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`She reaches for it. Abby's hand gets to the throttle first, and pulls it to idle. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your taxiway. You said it. We stop."</span>`,
          why:`Fixing a power problem while accelerating toward the end of the runway puts the decision after the decision point. The abort point exists so you don't have to decide on the roll. Reach it slow, and you reject the takeoff.`},
        {t:`Pull the power to idle and stop straight ahead on the runway.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Idle. Brakes. Nose dips. Centerline, mostly. She gets it off at the next taxiway with her pulse up where it lives on a three story carry down. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Centennial Tower, Cessna Niner One Echo, rejected takeoff, clear of the runway."</span> Tower says roger like she's asked for the time. On the taxiway she leans it out, full throttle, brakes on, and the tach comes up a hundred and twenty. She points at it. Abby nods. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"There you go. Screwed up, found out, still on the ground. That's the good version."</span> Second try, they're off well before the taxiway.`,
          why:`If the airplane isn't performing by the point you chose, reject the takeoff: power to idle, maximum braking while keeping directional control, and clear the runway. Then find the cause on the ground. Here it was full rich at a high field.`},
        {t:`Pull the carb heat on and keep the takeoff roll going.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Thirty six degrees, dewpoint three, wide open?"</span> Her hand's on the throttle already. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Carb heat right now just takes away more."</span>`,
          why:`Carb ice is most likely at low power. It's unlikely on a full throttle takeoff roll in air this hot and this dry. Carb heat reduces power further. The real problem is a full rich mixture, and the right move at the abort point is to stop.`},
        {t:`Keep going. With ten thousand feet of runway she'll get off eventually.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It does, eventually, and then it climbs like it's carrying a piano. On downwind Abby says, flat, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Ten thousand feet of runway did that. You didn't."</span>`,
          why:`On a very long runway it probably works out, which is why this isn't the worst choice. But the airplane is down on power, the climb will be poor, and you ignored the decision point you set. Stopping costs a minute.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s12`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.F`, title:`The scale`, q:`Two lines on the worksheet are blank, for Claire and Ellie. How should Jordan fill them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, 2:30, Claire's Subaru pulled up by the hangar. Ellie was out before it stopped. Shorts, soccer cleats (lucky, she said, they'd won twice in them), and Danny's old Hangar Six cap, on the last hole and still down over her eyebrows. Booster cushion under one arm. Claire had left the soft cooler of waters in the Subaru, and she had the face of somebody who'd talked herself into this for the whole drive up from Parker.`,`At three the ATIS said thirty five degrees, altimeter two niner eight two, wind variable at four. Jordan had the worksheet from Thursday on a clipboard. Two lines were blank.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your worksheet,"</span> Abby said. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"How are you getting their numbers?"</span>`,`Against the hangar wall sat Marisol's big flat scale on casters. Parts, luggage, and one time, for reasons nobody would explain to Jordan, a goat.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Weigh Claire and Ellie on Marisol's scale, dressed to fly.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Ellie thinks the airplane scale is the best thing that's happened all summer. 76 in cleats and the cap. Booster, 3. Claire gets on, gets off, says nothing. Abby, 150, as advertised. Jordan, 162 in boots. Bags and water, 20. Pencil, blank lines, done. From the bench Marisol says, mostly to the carburetor, <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"First time anybody's used that thing for the airplane."</span>`,
          why:`At the edge of the envelope, real weights matter. Weighing people as they'll fly, with shoes and gear, and weighing the baggage removes the guesswork. The PHAK says standard weights shouldn't be used when actual weights are available.`},
        {t:`Ask Claire and Ellie their weights, and add five pounds to each.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Claire gives a number. Ellie says how would she know, she's nine. Abby, low: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Scale's six feet away."</span>`,
          why:`Rounding up is the right instinct. But self reported weights are often low, they leave out shoes and gear, and a kid usually doesn't know. With a scale available, use it.`},
        {t:`Write in 170 for Claire and 80 for Ellie. Close enough for a lap.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Close enough for a lap."</span> She says it back slowly, like it's on a plaque. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Four of us. Three PM. August. Pick another flight to be close enough on."</span>`,
          why:`Estimated weights are fine for planning far from the limits. Four people in a 172 with full fuel on a 95 degree day is near the maximum weight, so a guess can put you over without your knowing.`},
        {t:`Leave them blank. She's sure four people won't reach max gross.`, s:0, flag:`skippedwb`, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Abby holds out a hand. Jordan gives her the clipboard. Abby looks at the two empty lines for a while and hands it back. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Sure isn't a number."</span> So Jordan weighs everybody, and the total isn't what she was sure of.`,
          why:`Being sure isn't a computation. A 172 with full fuel has roughly 570 pounds left for people and bags, which four people can use up fast. The only way to know is to weigh and compute.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f5s2`, title:`How high is Centennial today`,
     setup:[`Abby put both of Thursday's lines on the board and had Jordan work the morning and the afternoon side by side. Field elevation is 5,885 feet.`,`7 AM: <code>METAR KAPA 061253Z 18004KT 10SM SKC 17/07 A3006</code>`,`1 PM forecast: temperature 34°C, altimeter 29.90.`,`Use standard temperature = 15°C minus 2°C per 1,000 feet of pressure altitude, and DA ≈ PA + 120 × (OAT minus standard).`],
     fields:[{label:`Pressure altitude at 7 AM (feet)`,answer:5745,tol:0},{label:`Density altitude at 7 AM (feet)`,answer:7364,tol:15},{label:`Pressure altitude at 1 PM (feet)`,answer:5905,tol:0},{label:`Standard temperature at the 1 PM pressure altitude (°C)`,answer:3.19,tol:0.02},{label:`Density altitude at 1 PM (feet)`,answer:9602,tol:15},{label:`How much higher the airplane feels at 1 PM than at 7 AM (feet)`,answer:2238,tol:20}],
     solution:[`7 AM pressure altitude: 29.92 minus 30.06 = minus 0.14. × 1,000 = minus 140. 5,885 minus 140 = 5,745 feet.`,`7 AM standard temperature: 15 minus (2 × 5.745) = 15 minus 11.49 = 3.51°C. Difference: 17 minus 3.51 = 13.49. × 120 = 1,618.8. DA = 5,745 + 1,619 = about 7,364 feet.`,`1 PM pressure altitude: 29.92 minus 29.90 = plus 0.02. × 1,000 = plus 20. 5,885 + 20 = 5,905 feet.`,`1 PM standard temperature: 15 minus (2 × 5.905) = 15 minus 11.81 = 3.19°C.`,`1 PM density altitude: 34 minus 3.19 = 30.81. × 120 = 3,697.2. DA = 5,905 + 3,697 = about 9,602 feet.`,`Difference: 9,602 minus 7,364 = about 2,238 feet. Same airport, six hours apart. That's why Abby flies students at seven in the morning all summer.`]},
    {after:`f5s4`, title:`Two of us and forty gallons`,
     setup:[`Thursday's lesson. Use Biscuit's current weight and balance report and the arms from the type certificate and the POH.`,`Basic empty weight 1,486 lb at +39.5 (moment 58,697 lb-in). Front seats at +37.0: Jordan 162 lb, Abby 150 lb. Fuel 40 gallons usable at 6 lb per gallon, at +48.0. Baggage at +95.0: two flight bags and a gallon of water, 20 lb total. Maximum weight 2,300 lb.`,`CG limits: aft +47.3 at all weights. Forward +35.0 at 1,950 lb or less, +38.5 at 2,300 lb, straight line between.`],
     fields:[{label:`Total weight (lb)`,answer:2058,tol:0},{label:`Total moment (lb-in)`,answer:83661,tol:1},{label:`CG (inches aft of datum)`,answer:40.65,tol:0.01},{label:`Forward CG limit at this weight (inches)`,answer:36.08,tol:0.01},{label:`Weight still available before max gross (lb)`,answer:242,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Front seats: 162 + 150 = 312 lb. 312 × 37.0 = 11,544 lb-in.`,`Fuel: 40 × 6 = 240 lb. 240 × 48.0 = 11,520 lb-in.`,`Baggage: 20 × 95.0 = 1,900 lb-in.`,`Total weight: 1,486 + 312 + 240 + 20 = 2,058 lb. Total moment: 58,697 + 11,544 + 11,520 + 1,900 = 83,661 lb-in.`,`CG: 83,661 ÷ 2,058 = 40.65 inches.`,`Forward limit at 2,058 lb: the limit moves 3.5 inches over 350 pounds, which is 0.01 inch per pound. 2,058 minus 1,950 = 108 lb. 35.0 + (108 × 0.01) = 36.08 inches. The CG of 40.65 is between 36.08 and 47.3, so it's inside.`,`Room left: 2,300 minus 2,058 = 242 lb. Keep that number. Claire and Ellie have to fit inside it.`]},
    {after:`f5s5`, title:`Ground roll, with the wind`,
     setup:[`Thursday, 1 PM. Use Biscuit's takeoff table from the scene: the 2,100 lb block, the 6,000 foot row, and interpolate for 34°C.`,`The ATIS wind is 320 at 10 knots (magnetic). Runway 35R points 350 magnetic. Headwind component = wind × cos(angle). Crosswind component = wind × sin(angle).`,`Table note: decrease distances 10 percent for each 9 knots of headwind. Apply the note proportionally: 8.66 knots is 8.66/9 of a 10 percent step. Then apply Abby's rule: the 50 foot distance plus half again has to fit the runway.`],
     fields:[{label:`Headwind component (knots)`,answer:8.66,tol:0.02},{label:`Crosswind component (knots)`,answer:5,tol:0.02},{label:`Zero wind ground roll at 34°C (feet)`,answer:1164,tol:1},{label:`Zero wind total over 50 feet at 34°C (feet)`,answer:2165,tol:1},{label:`Ground roll corrected for headwind (feet)`,answer:1052,tol:2},{label:`Total over 50 feet corrected for headwind (feet)`,answer:1957,tol:2},{label:`Abby's number: corrected 50 foot distance plus 50 percent (feet)`,answer:2935,tol:3}],
     solution:[`Angle between wind and runway: 350 minus 320 = 30 degrees. Headwind = 10 × cos 30° = 10 × 0.866 = 8.66 knots. Crosswind = 10 × sin 30° = 10 × 0.5 = 5.0 knots, under the 8 knot limit Abby set for her.`,`Interpolation: 34°C is 4/10 of the way from 30 to 40, so 0.4.`,`Ground roll: 1,130 + 0.4 × (1,215 minus 1,130) = 1,130 + 0.4 × 85 = 1,130 + 34 = 1,164 feet.`,`Over 50 feet: 2,095 + 0.4 × (2,270 minus 2,095) = 2,095 + 0.4 × 175 = 2,095 + 70 = 2,165 feet.`,`Headwind correction: 8.66 ÷ 9 = 0.962. × 10 percent = 9.62 percent less. Multiply by 1 minus 0.0962 = 0.9038.`,`Ground roll: 1,164 × 0.9038 = 1,052 feet. Over 50 feet: 2,165 × 0.9038 = 1,957 feet.`,`Abby's margin: 1,957 × 1.5 = 2,935 feet. Runway 35R is 10,001 feet. On runway 28 it would be 4,800. Both fit today. Notice how little the 9 knot headwind gave back, about 200 feet, and how much the heat took.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f5s7`, title:`Better or worse`, intro:`On the way back from the ramp Abby handed Jordan a sheet with one change per line. For each one, does takeoff distance get longer, shorter, or stay the same? Everything else held equal.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`The altimeter setting drops from 30.10 to 29.80.`, choices:[`Longer`,`Shorter`,`No change`], answer:0, why:`Lower pressure raises pressure altitude and density altitude, so performance gets worse.`},
      {prompt:`The temperature drops from 34°C to 20°C.`, choices:[`Longer`,`Shorter`,`No change`], answer:1, why:`Cooler air is denser. Density altitude goes down and the takeoff gets shorter.`},
      {prompt:`Taking off with a 4 knot tailwind instead of calm wind.`, choices:[`Longer`,`Shorter`,`No change`], answer:0, why:`Biscuit's note adds 10 percent for each 2 knots of tailwind. Four knots adds about 20 percent.`},
      {prompt:`The relative humidity rises from 10 percent to 80 percent.`, choices:[`Longer`,`Shorter`,`No change`], answer:0, why:`Moist air is less dense than dry air, so density altitude rises a little and performance drops. There's no chart for it, so expect a little worse than the book.`},
      {prompt:`Taking off from a dry grass runway instead of pavement.`, choices:[`Longer`,`Shorter`,`No change`], answer:0, why:`Biscuit's note adds 15 percent of the ground roll figure for dry grass.`},
      {prompt:`Adding 200 pounds of people to the back seats.`, choices:[`Longer`,`Shorter`,`No change`], answer:0, why:`More weight needs a higher lift-off speed and accelerates slower. About 10 percent more weight costs at least 21 percent more distance.`},
      {prompt:`A runway that slopes downhill in the direction of takeoff.`, choices:[`Longer`,`Shorter`,`No change`], answer:1, why:`A downslope helps acceleration and shortens the takeoff. It lengthens a landing in the same direction.`},
      {prompt:`Taking off with the mixture full rich at Centennial instead of leaned for max RPM.`, choices:[`Longer`,`Shorter`,`No change`], answer:0, why:`At a high field, full rich is too rich and costs power. The POH note says to lean for max RPM before takeoff above 3,000 feet.`},
      {prompt:`Lifting off at the book speed indicated, on a hot day versus a cold day.`, choices:[`Same groundspeed at lift-off`,`Higher groundspeed at lift-off on the hot day`,`Lower groundspeed at lift-off on the hot day`], answer:1, why:`The indicated lift-off speed is the same, but the true airspeed is higher in thin air, so the groundspeed at lift-off is higher and the roll is longer.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Field elevation is 5,885 feet and the altimeter setting is 29.82. The pressure altitude is approximately`, opts:[`5,785 feet`,`5,985 feet`,`6,085 feet`], a:1, why:`Pressure altitude = 5,885 + (29.92 minus 29.82) × 1,000 = 5,885 + 100 = 5,985 feet. A setting below 29.92 raises pressure altitude above field elevation.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`At a pressure altitude of 6,000 feet, the outside air temperature is 33°C. Using the rule of thumb, the density altitude is about`, opts:[`9,600 feet`,`8,160 feet`,`10,000 feet`], a:0, why:`Standard at 6,000 feet is 15 minus 12 = 3°C. 33 minus 3 = 30. 30 × 120 = 3,600. 6,000 + 3,600 = 9,600 feet. Using 15°C as standard gives 8,160, which is the classic mistake.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`What effect does high relative humidity have on aircraft performance?`, opts:[`It increases performance, because moist air is denser`,`It has no effect, because humidity isn't part of density altitude`,`It decreases performance, because moist air is less dense`], a:2, why:`Water vapor is lighter than dry air, so humid air is less dense. Density altitude rises and performance drops. The PHAK says there's no rule of thumb for it, but it must be considered.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A POH table gives a total takeoff distance of 2,000 feet with zero wind. The note says decrease distances 10 percent for each 9 knots of headwind. With an 18 knot headwind, the distance is`, opts:[`1,800 feet`,`1,600 feet`,`1,640 feet`], a:1, why:`18 knots is two 9 knot steps, so 20 percent less. 2,000 × 0.80 = 1,600 feet. The tailwind correction in the same notes is much larger per knot.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`According to the PHAK, a 10 percent increase in takeoff gross weight results in at least a`, opts:[`10 percent increase in takeoff distance`,`21 percent increase in takeoff distance`,`5 percent decrease in rate of acceleration`], a:1, why:`A 10 percent weight increase gives about a 5 percent higher takeoff speed, at least a 9 percent lower acceleration, and at least a 21 percent increase in takeoff distance.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`An airplane weighs 2,080 pounds with a total moment of 86,220 pound inches. The CG is located at`, opts:[`+40.2 inches`,`+42.6 inches`,`+41.5 inches`], a:2, why:`CG = total moment ÷ total weight = 86,220 ÷ 2,080 = 41.45, about +41.5 inches aft of the datum.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A 2,300 pound airplane's CG must move 1.0 inch forward. How much baggage must be moved from the baggage area at +95 to the rear seat at +73?`, opts:[`About 105 pounds`,`About 46 pounds`,`About 63 pounds`], a:0, why:`Weight shifted = total weight × ΔCG ÷ distance shifted = 2,300 × 1.0 ÷ 22 = 104.5 pounds. This is the FAA-H-8083-1B weight shift formula rearranged.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Compared with an aft CG, an airplane loaded with a forward CG will`, opts:[`stall at a lower airspeed`,`stall at a higher airspeed`,`be less stable in pitch`], a:1, why:`With a forward CG, the tail must push down harder, which adds to the wing's load. The stalling angle of attack is reached at a higher speed. Forward CG is more stable, not less.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Loading an airplane with the CG aft of the aft limit is hazardous mainly because it`, opts:[`makes stall and spin recovery more difficult`,`causes excessive loads on the nose wheel`,`increases the stalling speed sharply`], a:0, why:`An aft CG reduces longitudinal stability, lightens control forces, and makes stall and spin recovery harder, possibly impossible. Nose wheel loads and higher stall speeds go with a forward CG.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`An airplane lifted off below its recommended speed on a hot, high day may be unable to climb because`, opts:[`ground effect increases induced drag near the runway`,`the airspeed indicator reads too high in ground effect`,`induced drag increases as it climbs out of ground effect`], a:2, why:`Ground effect reduces induced drag near the surface. As the airplane climbs out of it, induced drag rises. At high density altitude it may fly in ground effect but not climb out. Lower the nose and accelerate first.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`According to the PHAK, landing 10 percent faster than the recommended approach speed increases landing distance by at least`, opts:[`21 percent`,`5 percent`,`10 percent`], a:0, why:`Excess speed adds kinetic energy that the brakes have to remove. A 10 percent excess landing speed causes at least a 21 percent increase in landing distance.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Before takeoff, the AFH recommends identifying a point along the runway where the airplane should be airborne. If that point is reached on the ground, the pilot should`, opts:[`continue the takeoff, since most of the runway is still ahead`,`reduce power to idle, brake, and keep the airplane straight`,`apply full carburetor heat and continue the roll to lift-off`], a:1, why:`If the airplane isn't airborne by the chosen point, reject the takeoff: power to idle, maximum braking, and keep it straight. The point is chosen so the stop fits on the remaining runway.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, weather. The surface temperature is 32°C and the dewpoint is 12°C. The approximate base of cumulus clouds is`, opts:[`8,000 feet MSL`,`4,500 feet AGL`,`8,000 feet AGL`], a:2, why:`Spread = 20°C. 20 ÷ 2.5 × 1,000 = 8,000 feet. The estimate gives height above the ground, so it's AGL.`, task:`I.C`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, airspace. A Class D ceiling is charted in a dashed box as minus 80. This means the Class D extends from the surface up to`, opts:[`but not including 8,000 feet MSL`,`8,000 feet AGL, the top of the surface area`,`and including 8,000 feet MSL`], a:0, why:`Class D ceilings are in hundreds of feet MSL. The minus sign means up to but not including that altitude, usually because Class B or C sits on top.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, systems. As an airplane with a carburetor climbs without the mixture being adjusted, the fuel and air mixture becomes`, opts:[`leaner, because the fuel flow drops with altitude`,`unchanged, because the carburetor compensates`,`richer, because the air gets less dense`], a:2, why:`The carburetor meters fuel by volume of air. As density drops, the same volume of air weighs less while the fuel doesn't, so the mixture gets richer. That's why you lean for max RPM before takeoff at a high field.`, task:`I.G`, domain:`Preflight`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `She did the worksheet on the hood of Claire's Subaru. The briefing room was a hundred yards off and losing its own fight with the heat. Everybody stood there and watched her, and she hated it. Pencil. One line, then the next. No talking. Like charting a code after.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Biscuit weight and balance, Saturday August 15, 3 PM">ITEM / WEIGHT / ARM / MOMENT<br>Basic empty (Marisol, Oct 2019): 1,486 / 39.5 / 58,697<br>Front seats, Jordan 162, Abby 150: 312 / 37.0 / 11,544<br>Rear seats, Claire 174, Ellie 76, booster 3: 253 / 73.0 / 18,469<br>Fuel, 40 gal usable (Russ topped off at noon): 240 / 48.0 / 11,520<br>Baggage, 2 flight bags, 1 gal water: 20 / 95.0 / 1,900<br>TOTAL: 2,311 / CG 44.19 / 102,130<br>MAX 2,300. <b>OVER BY 11.</b><br><br>Without baggage or water: 2,291 / CG 43.75 / 100,230. Under by 9.<br>PA 5,985. Standard 3.0°C. OAT 35°C. <b>DA about 9,820.</b><br>Takeoff, 2,300 block, 6,000 row, 35°C, no wind: 1,443 ground roll, 2,655 over 50 ft.<br>Climb, 2,300 lb, 6,000 ft, 35°C: about 410 fpm.</prop>`,
    `She went back to the top and added it again. Same number. Of course.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Talk me through it,"</span> Abby said. She wasn't going to make the call. She'd said so Tuesday night, more or less, and she meant it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"We're eleven over. If we leave every bag and the water on the ramp, we're nine under. Nine pounds. On the hottest day this summer, at almost ten thousand feet of density altitude, which is the worst Biscuit can do. Four hundred feet a minute up, through the bumps, at seventy five hundred, which is sixteen hundred feet over Parker with the cumulus going up."</span> She heard herself and kept going. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Quiet, certain, deciding out loud.">"The runway's fine. The runway isn't the problem. It's legal by nine pounds and a jug of water, and I'd be doing it because it's legal."</span>`,
    `Nobody said anything. Out on the ramp the heat came up off the concrete in sheets and made the hangars across the way wobble. Somewhere a Cirrus was doing its run up and it sounded tired too.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I'd fly it,"</span> Abby said, finally. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Legally. I've flown worse. And I'd spend the whole climb with my hand next to the throttle, wishing I'd said no in the parking lot."</span> She looked at Jordan. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your numbers. Your call."</span>`,
    `Jordan wrote one more line at the bottom of the sheet. <b>SEPT, 7 AM, 14°C, A3010, 20 GAL: 2,191 LB WITH BAGS, CG 43.98, DA ABOUT 6,950.</b> She'd worked it out last night, in case, and hadn't told anybody, because doing the math for the no felt like deciding it.`,
    `Then she crouched down in front of Ellie, on the hot concrete, so they were eye to eye under the brim of the cap.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Gentle, soft, steady.">"Not today, El."</span>`,
    `Ellie looked at her, then at the airplane, then at her mother, then back. She'd measured herself on the pantry door. She'd worn her lucky cleats. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie" data-tone="Small, confused, pleading.">"But it's legal. You said."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Gentle, soft, steady.">"It's legal. Barely. You know how Biscuit gets out of breath when it's hot? Today she'd be out of breath with all four of us and the gas, the whole way up. I don't want your first time to be her worst day. I want it to be her best one."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ellie" data-tone="Hurt, on the edge of tears.">"You promised."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Gentle, soft, steady.">"Your dad promised when you were tall enough. You're tall enough. It's the airplane that isn't ready today."</span>`,
    `It didn't help. Ellie's chin went first. Jordan had seen that on a lot of kids in the back of the rig. Then Ellie was crying, hard, and Danny's cap fell off on the concrete, and she stepped on it with a cleat and didn't notice. Claire picked her up. Ellie was too big to carry and Claire carried her anyway, and looked at Jordan over Ellie's head.`,
    (s.score>=27
      ?`Jordan expected anger. She'd have understood it. What she got was a nod. Small. The same one Claire gave doctors in the ER, Jordan figured, when they said the thing nobody wanted to hear and said it right.`
      :`Jordan expected anger, and after the last two weeks she wasn't sure she hadn't earned some. What she got was a nod. Small. The same one Claire gave doctors in the ER, Jordan figured, when they said the thing nobody wanted to hear and said it right.`),
    (s.flags.skippedwb||G.skippedwb)
      ?`Later Jordan would think about the shortcut she'd reached for this week, and how close this afternoon had come to being decided by a feeling instead of a number.`
      :`Later Jordan would think about how easy it would have been to guess. Four people in a 172, a kid, a lap around the airport. Every guess she could have made would have been a little light.`,
    `Claire carried Ellie to the car and sat in the back seat with her with the door open and the engine running for the air. Abby went inside without saying anything, which was the kindest thing anyone did all afternoon. Marisol put the scale back against the wall and put the booster cushion on top of it, carefully, like it was going to be needed.`,
    `Jordan walked out to Biscuit. She took the sunshade down and opened the door and sat in the left seat with the door open, the vinyl hot enough through her jeans to make her jump. By the mag switch, Ellie's sticker: HI DAD, in purple marker, the corners curling up in the heat. She took the green notebook out of her bag. She'd been reading it in order, mostly. She didn't do that today. She went looking for August, and found it.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Danny's logbook, a page from August, ten years ago">August 28. KAPA local, rental 172 from the school, 0.6. 94 degrees. Me, Claire, Tomás and Bree from Claire's work in back, full tanks, cooler in the baggage, because we were going to fly over Cherry Creek at sunset and it was going to be great. Walt was at the fuel truck and asked what we weighed. I said "not much." Ha. Asked for 28 because it was right there. Used most of it. MOST OF IT. 4,800 feet and the wheels came off somewhere past where I could see the end, and then she just hung there over the fence and thought about it. Climbed like a shopping cart. Took forever to get to pattern altitude. Claire held my knee the whole time and didn't say anything, which was worse than yelling. Back on the ground I did the math I should have done before. Over gross. Didn't lean either. New rule: W&B on paper every time there's more than two of us. If it's hot, do it twice. Claire says don't write down that she was there because she's not sure she was supposed to be flying. I said I don't write anything down. (+1 aboard. She doesn't know I know. Due in April.)</prop>`,
    `Jordan read it twice, and then a third time on the last line, and then she sat with her hand flat on the page for a long time.`,
    `Ten years ago in August. Due in April. Ellie had been in the back of an overloaded 172 on a 94 degree day, hanging over the fence on runway 28, before she was anybody. Before Danny was anybody's dad. He'd known. He'd written it in parentheses.`,
    `The right door opened. Claire, folding herself in like the seat might bite. She pulled the door to but didn't latch it. Red eyes, dry now. <span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Quiet, warm, clear.">"Out cold. Cried herself to sleep in the back seat. Marisol's in the car with her, doing a crossword out loud."</span> She looked at the notebook. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Which one's that?"</span>`,
    `Jordan handed it over, open. Claire read. Jordan could tell exactly when she hit the parentheses. Hand over the mouth, like at the lawyer's office in April. Then down again.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Claire">"He knew,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"I hadn't told him. I had a card picked out."</span> The short laugh. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"I remember that fence. I didn't get in one for ten years. Today was going to be the first time. He wrote that rule. I never saw him do it on the hood of a car."</span> She held the notebook on her knees with both hands. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"He'd have taken her today. It probably would've been fine."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Probably."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Quiet, warm, clear.">"Probably fine. We chart that at work. It's not a vital sign."</span> She gave the notebook back. <span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Quiet, warm, clear.">"September, seven AM. I read the bottom of your sheet."</span> She got out, then leaned back in with one hand on the roof. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Bring the scale."</span>`,
    `At 9:40 that night, in Aurora, the phone buzzed twice in a row.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Lourdes Reyes" time="9:40 PM">Claire told me. You told the baby no. Good. I am proud of you mija. I am still not getting in that airplane.</prop>`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="9:41 PM">Right call. Next is pattern work. Crosswinds, go arounds, slips, and you landing it every time. Walt says he's coming to watch. Ignore him. You won't be able to.</prop>`,
    `She read both of them standing at the counter in her socks. Then she got the legal pad out of her bag, turned to a clean page, and wrote SEPTEMBER at the top of it, and under that, in her own small handwriting, the weights she'd measured that afternoon, every one of them, so she wouldn't have to ask anybody again.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch05.mp3`, len:`about 55 minutes`},
  next:`Pattern Work`
});
